---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import DemoFrame from "../../components/DemoFrame.astro";
import RelatedLink from "../../components/RelatedLink.astro";

const HELPER_OPTIONS = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12];
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/distributed-computing"
	description="Crowdsourcing and distributed computing explained: split one huge job across many helpers, watch the time drop, and see where the extra helpers stop paying off."
	lead="Some jobs are too big for one person — or one computer. So split them up and hand out the pieces."
>
	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Many Hands</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Imagine counting every bird in a whole country on a single day. One person could never do it. But
			thousands of volunteers, each counting in their own neighborhood and sending in their numbers,
			can — and bird-watching groups really do run counts like this every year.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Computers use the same idea. When a job is too big for one machine, it's split into pieces and
			shared out across many machines connected by a network. Each one works on its own piece, and the
			results are combined at the end.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Three Related Ideas</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data-table">
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><em>Parallel computing</em></td>
						<td>One computer with several processors working on parts of a job at the same time.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><em>Distributed computing</em></td>
						<td>Many separate computers, connected by a network, sharing one big job.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><em>Crowdsourcing</em></td>
						<td>Many <em>people</em> each contributing a small piece — often volunteers, often online.</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<DemoFrame label="Distributed computing demo">
		<div class="dc-demo" data-dc-demo data-helper-options={JSON.stringify(HELPER_OPTIONS)}>
			<p class="dc-task">The job: count every <span class="mono-highlight">1</span> in this list of 120 bits.</p>

			<div class="demo-field">
				<label for="dc-helpers">Helpers</label>
				<input id="dc-helpers" class="demo-range dc-helpers" type="range" min="0" max={HELPER_OPTIONS.length - 1} value="0" data-dc-helpers />
				<output for="dc-helpers" class="dc-helpers-value" data-dc-helpers-value>1</output>
			</div>

			<div class="demo-field dc-predict">
				<label for="dc-predict">Predict first: how many seconds will it take?</label>
				<input id="dc-predict" class="demo-input dc-predict-input" type="number" min="0" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="optional" data-dc-predict />
			</div>

			<div class="dc-workers" data-dc-workers></div>

			<div class="demo-stats">
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-dc-timer>0.0 s</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Time</span>
				</div>
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-dc-total>—</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Ones found</span>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="demo-status" data-dc-status aria-live="polite">Pick how many helpers, then press Start.</p>

			<div class="demo-controls">
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn demo-btn--primary" data-dc-start>Start</button>
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn" data-dc-new>New list</button>
			</div>

			<div class="dc-history" data-dc-history-wrap hidden>
				<p class="dc-history-title">Your runs</p>
				<table class="dc-history-table">
					<thead>
						<tr><th>Helpers</th><th>Time</th><th>Speedup</th></tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody data-dc-history></tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="demo-note">Each bit takes a helper 0.1 seconds to check. Handing out the work and adding up everyone's answers takes a little time too — more helpers, more to organize.</p>
		</div>
	</DemoFrame>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">What You Probably Noticed</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>
				<em>More helpers, less time</em> — at first, dramatically. Two helpers nearly halve the time.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>But the gains shrink.</em> Every helper has to be handed their piece and report back, and
				that organizing takes time that doesn't get shared. With lots of helpers, the organizing starts to
				cancel out the benefit.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Everyone waits for the slowest helper.</em> The job isn't done until the last piece is
				finished.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="block-text">
			These are exactly the ideas from the Speedup page — here you can see them happen.
		</p>
		<RelatedLink href="/learn/speedup" label="The ideas behind it" />
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Real-World Examples</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>
				<em>Search engines</em> spread the job of searching the web across thousands of computers, which
				is how you get answers in a fraction of a second.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Volunteer computing</em> projects let people donate their home computer's spare time to
				science — for example, simulating how proteins fold to help medical research.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Citizen science</em> websites ask volunteers to sort through photos of galaxies, animals caught
				on wildlife cameras, or old handwritten records — work computers still find tricky.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Online encyclopedias and map apps</em> are crowdsourced: millions of people add facts,
				fix mistakes, and report traffic jams.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Not Every Job Splits Up</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Counting works beautifully because each piece is independent — helper 3 doesn't need to know what
			helper 7 found. Some jobs aren't like that. Baking a cake: you can't ice it before it's baked, no
			matter how many bakers you have. When each step depends on the one before, extra helpers can't do
			much.
		</p>
		<p class="block-callout">
			Distributed computing shines when a big job can be broken into lots of independent pieces.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			It also solves a second problem: size. Some jobs involve more data than any single computer could
			store, or would take one computer years to finish. Spreading the data and the work across many
			machines makes those problems possible at all — not just faster.
		</p>
	</section>
</LearnArticle>

<script>
	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-demo]")!;
	const OPTIONS: number[] = JSON.parse(root.dataset.helperOptions!);
	const helpersInput = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-dc-helpers]")!;
	const helpersValue = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-helpers-value]")!;
	const workersEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-workers]")!;
	const timerEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-timer]")!;
	const totalEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-total]")!;
	const statusEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-status]")!;
	const startBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-dc-start]")!;
	const newBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-dc-new]")!;
	const historyWrap = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-history-wrap]")!;
	const historyEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-dc-history]")!;
	const predictInput = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-dc-predict]")!;

	const N = 120;
	const SECONDS_PER_BIT = 0.1;
	const OVERHEAD_PER_HELPER = 0.1; // half to hand out work, half to combine results
	const REAL_MS_PER_SIM_SECOND = 350; // 1 helper ≈ 4 seconds of animation

	let bits: number[] = [];
	let helpers = 1;
	let frame = 0;
	let running = false;
	const runs: { helpers: number; time: number }[] = [];

	function newBits() {
		bits = Array.from({ length: N }, () => (Math.random() < 0.45 ? 1 : 0));
	}

	/** Contiguous, equal-as-possible chunks — one per helper. */
	function chunks(count: number): number[][] {
		const out: number[][] = [];
		const size = Math.ceil(N / count);
		for (let start = 0; start < N; start += size) {
			out.push(Array.from({ length: Math.min(size, N - start) }, (_, i) => start + i));
		}
		return out;
	}

	function totalTime(count: number) {
		return count * OVERHEAD_PER_HELPER + Math.ceil(N / count) * SECONDS_PER_BIT;
	}

	function buildWorkers() {
		workersEl.innerHTML = "";
		workersEl.dataset.count = String(helpers);
		chunks(helpers).forEach((indices, h) => {
			const card = document.createElement("div");
			card.className = "dc-worker";
			const head = document.createElement("p");
			head.className = "dc-worker-head";
			head.innerHTML = `<span>Helper ${h + 1}</span><span class="dc-worker-count" data-dc-count="${h}"></span>`;
			const grid = document.createElement("div");
			grid.className = "dc-bits";
			grid.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
			indices.forEach((i) => {
				const tile = document.createElement("span");
				tile.className = "dc-bit";
				tile.dataset.index = String(i);
				tile.textContent = String(bits[i]);
				grid.append(tile);
			});
			card.append(head, grid);
			workersEl.append(card);
		});
	}

	function setHelpers() {
		helpers = OPTIONS[Number(helpersInput.value)];
		helpersValue.textContent = String(helpers);
		if (!running) {
			buildWorkers();
			timerEl.textContent = "0.0 s";
			totalEl.textContent = "—";
		}
	}

	function renderHistory() {
		historyWrap.hidden = runs.length === 0;
		historyEl.innerHTML = "";
		const baseline = totalTime(1);
		[...runs]
			.sort((a, b) => a.helpers - b.helpers)
			.forEach((run) => {
				const tr = document.createElement("tr");
				tr.innerHTML = `<td>${run.helpers}</td><td>${run.time.toFixed(1)} s</td><td>${(baseline / run.time).toFixed(1)}×</td>`;
				historyEl.append(tr);
			});
	}

	function start() {
		if (running) return;
		running = true;
		startBtn.disabled = true;
		newBtn.disabled = true;
		helpersInput.disabled = true;
		predictInput.disabled = true;
		buildWorkers();

		const parts = chunks(helpers);
		const splitTime = (helpers * OVERHEAD_PER_HELPER) / 2;
		const workTime = Math.ceil(N / helpers) * SECONDS_PER_BIT;
		const finishTime = totalTime(helpers);
		const tiles = Array.from(workersEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".dc-bit"));
		const tileByIndex = new Map(tiles.map((t) => [Number(t.dataset.index), t]));
		const t0 = performance.now();
		statusEl.textContent = helpers === 1 ? "Working through the list alone…" : "Handing out the work…";

		const tick = (now: number) => {
			const sim = Math.min(finishTime, (now - t0) / REAL_MS_PER_SIM_SECOND);
			timerEl.textContent = `${sim.toFixed(1)} s`;
			const checked = Math.max(0, Math.floor((sim - splitTime) / SECONDS_PER_BIT));

			parts.forEach((indices, h) => {
				const done = Math.min(indices.length, checked);
				let ones = 0;
				indices.forEach((index, k) => {
					const tile = tileByIndex.get(index)!;
					tile.classList.toggle("is-checked", k < done);
					tile.classList.toggle("is-current", k === done && done < indices.length && sim >= splitTime);
					if (k < done) ones += bits[index];
				});
				const countEl = workersEl.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-dc-count="${h}"]`)!;
				countEl.textContent = sim >= splitTime ? `${ones} found` : "";
			});

			if (sim >= splitTime + workTime && sim < finishTime && helpers > 1) {
				statusEl.textContent = "Adding up everyone's answers…";
			} else if (sim >= splitTime && sim < splitTime + workTime && helpers > 1) {
				statusEl.textContent = `${helpers} helpers counting at the same time…`;
			}

			if (sim < finishTime) {
				frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
				return;
			}
			const total = bits.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
			totalEl.textContent = String(total);
			runs.push({ helpers, time: finishTime });
			renderHistory();
			const guess = Number(predictInput.value);
			const predictionNote =
				predictInput.value.trim() !== "" && Number.isFinite(guess)
					? Math.abs(guess - finishTime) <= 0.3
						? ` You predicted ${guess} s — spot on!`
						: ` You predicted ${guess} s (${guess > finishTime ? "slower" : "faster"} than it really was).`
					: "";
			statusEl.textContent = `Done: ${total} ones counted in ${finishTime.toFixed(1)} seconds with ${helpers} helper${helpers === 1 ? "" : "s"}.${predictionNote} Try a different number of helpers.`;
			predictInput.value = "";
			running = false;
			startBtn.disabled = false;
			newBtn.disabled = false;
			helpersInput.disabled = false;
			predictInput.disabled = false;
			startBtn.textContent = "Run again";
		};
		frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
	}

	helpersInput.addEventListener("input", setHelpers);
	startBtn.addEventListener("click", start);
	newBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
		cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
		newBits();
		buildWorkers();
		timerEl.textContent = "0.0 s";
		totalEl.textContent = "—";
		statusEl.textContent = "A fresh list of bits. Press Start.";
	});

	newBits();
	setHelpers();
</script>

<style is:global>
	.dc-demo .dc-predict-input {
		width: 7em;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-task {
		margin: 0 0 0.3em;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 1em;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-helpers {
		width: min(16em, 60%);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-helpers-value {
		min-width: 1.6em;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.4em;
		color: #fff3d6;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-workers {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.5em;
		margin-top: 0.9em;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-worker {
		padding: 0.45em 0.5em 0.55em;
		border-radius: 10px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		background: var(--color-bg);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-workers[data-count="1"] .dc-worker {
		width: 100%;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-worker-head {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.6em;
		margin: 0 0 0.35em;
		font-size: 0.78em;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-worker-count {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-bits {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.2em;
		max-width: 16em;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-workers[data-count="1"] .dc-bits {
		max-width: none;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-bit {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		width: 1.45em;
		height: 1.45em;
		border-radius: 4px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		background: var(--color-surface);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.8em;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	/* A checked bit just fades back — every bit gets the same treatment, so
	   the only "signal" is how far each helper has got. */
	.dc-demo .dc-bit.is-checked {
		opacity: 0.35;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-bit.is-current {
		border-color: var(--color-accent);
		box-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 60%, transparent);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-history {
		margin: 1em auto 0;
		max-width: 22em;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-history-title {
		margin: 0 0 0.3em;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 0.78em;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-history-table {
		width: 100%;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.92em;
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-history-table th {
		padding: 0.3em 0.6em;
		font-family: var(--font-sans);
		font-size: 0.8em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.05em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.dc-demo .dc-history-table td {
		padding: 0.3em 0.6em;
		text-align: center;
		color: var(--color-text);
		border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-border) 45%, transparent);
	}
</style>
